@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Intellectual Development and the Periodization Debate
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-17
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 9, Medium: 8, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef, WebSearch (fallback for publisher verification)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The literature on Lukács's intellectual biography is organized around a
recurring meta-question: how many "Lukácses" were there, and what, if
anything, connects them? The classic biographical scholarship of the
late 1970s and 1980s established the terrain. Löwy (1979/2023) reads
Lukács's trajectory from the Sunday Circle romanticism of Soul and Form
through The Theory of the Novel to the 1918 conversion to Bolshevism as
a coherent evolution of "romantic anti-capitalism" into revolutionary
Marxism, not a rupture. Arato and Breines (1979) likewise treat the
pre-Marxist and Marxist Lukács as continuous, tracing how concerns
inherited from Simmel, Weber, and neo-Kantianism (alienation, "second
nature," the problem of form) resurface, transformed, in the theory of
reification. Congdon (1983) and Gluck (1985), working from the Lukács
Archive materials discovered in 1972, offer more skeptical,
biographically grounded accounts that stress contingency, personal
crisis (the suicide of Irma Seidler), and the specific milieu of the
Budapest Sunday Circle rather than a philosophically necessary
evolution toward communism. Kadarkay's (1991) comprehensive biography
synthesizes archival material into a full life-and-politics narrative
and is a standard reference point for later scholarship, including
the SEP entry itself.

A second cluster of literature treats the "conversion" moment (1918-19)
directly as a problem in the history of political thought: Nakhimovsky
(2022) argues against reading "Bolshevism as a Moral Problem" and
"Tactics and Ethics" merely as autobiographical conversion narratives,
situating them instead within a Weberian-Fichtean tradition of
"Realpolitik" reasoning about ethically responsible revolutionary
action. Westerman (2017) traces the specific role of Dostoevsky
reception in mediating Lukács's shift from ethical individualism to
revolutionary ethics. Lendvai (2008) offers a detailed intellectual-
historical reconstruction of the 1902-1918 period ("his way to Marx").

A third cluster addresses the 1920s-1930s "Moscow" phase and the
question of Stalinist accommodation: Congdon (2007) examines the
unpublished 1925-26 manuscript "Tailism and the Dialectic" (a defense
of History and Class Consciousness against party critics) as evidence
of Lukács's growing self-subordination to party orthodoxy; Tihanov
(2022) situates Lukács's Moscow exile alongside Béla Balázs to
illuminate the institutional and existential conditions of "exilic
Marxism" in the Stalin period.

The 1967 preface to History and Class Consciousness is treated by the
literature as the pivotal document for adjudicating the "break vs.
continuity" question, because it is Lukács's own retrospective
self-periodization. Baehrens (2023a, 2023b), in a paired set of essays
marking the HCC centenary, reads the 1922 and 1967 prefaces against one
another as two different "agenda-setting" framings of the book, while
Macciò (1972) represents the earlier Telos-circle debate about how to
read "the late Lukács" against his own self-criticism. Forczek (2023)
extends the periodization question into Lukács's final, unfinished
project, the Ontology of Social Being, arguing that the disciples'
("Budapest School") charge of an internal inconsistency in the late
work rests on a misreading. Finally, Gilbert (2020), Perica (2020), and
Markus (1983/2011) - representing more recent "continuity thesis"
scholarship growing out of Lukács-studies venues (Zagreber Germanistische
Beiträge's dedicated "young Lukács" issue) - argue against sharp
epistemological-break models by tracing single problems (subjectivism,
form/content, ethics) across the pre-Marxist/Marxist divide.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the biographical and developmental scaffolding
that any account of "Lukács's philosophy of history" must presuppose:
whether the theory of history elaborated in History and Class
Consciousness (and revised in the Ontology) is continuous with the
philosophy-of-history concerns already present in The Theory of the
Novel (loss of totality, "transcendental homelessness"), or whether it
marks a genuine rupture produced by the encounter with Marx and
Hegel. The periodization debate directly conditions how later
domains of the review (e.g., analyses of reification, totality, and
the critique of historicism) should weight early versus late texts as
evidence for Lukács's "own" view.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The literature is heavily weighted toward the pre-1930 material (the
"young Lukács" industry); systematic treatments of the periodization
question that give equal weight to the post-1945 aesthetic and
ontological writings are comparatively rare (Forczek 2023 and Congdon
2007 are partial exceptions). English-language scholarship on the
Moscow-period writings (1930-1945) remains thin relative to Hungarian-
and German-language literature. Several classic biographical monographs
(Löwy, Arato & Breines, Congdon, Gluck) predate the DOI/abstract
infrastructure of modern academic databases and could not be enriched
with machine-readable abstracts; these are marked INCOMPLETE below but
remain bibliographically verified and central to the domain.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize the synthesis section around the three interpretive models
identified above (rupture/epistemological break; continuity-of-problem;
tactical-self-periodization) rather than strict chronology, and use the
1967 preface as the hinge text connecting this domain to any subsequent
discussion of Lukács's mature theory of history.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Rupture/epistemological-break model: represented in the literature's
  framing of a "Lukács question" (via reviews of Löwy/Arato-Breines) and
  discussed critically by Perica (2020) and Gilbert (2020), who argue
  against it.
- Continuity-of-problem model: Löwy (1979/2023), Arato & Breines (1979),
  Gilbert (2020), Perica (2020), Markus (1983/2011) - 5 works locating
  persistent concerns (alienation, form, totality) across the divide.
- Biographical/contingency model: Congdon (1983), Gluck (1985), Kadarkay
  (1991) - stress specific personal and historical circumstance over
  philosophical necessity.
- Self-periodization-as-tactics model: Congdon (2007), Baehrens (2023a,
  2023b), Macciò (1972) - treat Lukács's own retrospective framings
  (Tailism, 1967 preface) as strategic interventions requiring critical
  reading rather than face-value acceptance.
====================================================================
}

@misc{stahl2013lukacs,
  author = {Stahl, Titus},
  title = {Georg [György] Lukács},
  year = {2013},
  howpublished = {\url{https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lukacs/}},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This Stanford Encyclopedia entry provides the standard reference biography and philosophical overview of Lukács, narrating the trajectory from the Budapest and Heidelberg years (Soul and Form, Theory of the Novel, the unpublished "Heidelberg Aesthetics") through the 1918 Communist Party membership, the 1919 Hungarian Soviet Republic, History and Class Consciousness (1923), the Moscow exile and public retraction of HCC (1933), to the late Aesthetics and Ontology of Social Being. It flags that the degree of Lukács's genuine agreement with Stalinism "is disputed to this day," citing Lichtheim, Deutscher, Kolakowski, and Pike as representing different positions.

  RELEVANCE: Functions as the master timeline and bibliographic index against which all periodization arguments in this domain are checked; its citation of unresolved disputes (Stalinism, sincerity of self-criticism) is itself evidence that periodization remains a live interpretive problem rather than a settled biographical fact.

  POSITION: Reference/overview; implicitly favors a continuity reading by tracing single concepts (totality, reification, anti-positivism) across periods without asserting an epistemological break.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, biography, encyclopedia, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{lowy2023lukacs,
  author = {Löwy, Michael},
  title = {Georg Lukács: From Romanticism to Bolshevism},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Löwy argues that Lukács's turn to Bolshevism was not an abandonment of his pre-Marxist "romantic anti-capitalist" sensibility but its dialectical continuation: the same revulsion against the "disenchantment" and reification of bourgeois modernity that animated Soul and Form and The Theory of the Novel is redirected, after 1917-18, toward revolutionary politics rather than aesthetic or ethical withdrawal. Löwy reconstructs this shift through close reading of Lukács's Sunday Circle milieu, his Weberian and neo-Kantian formation, and the specific political conjuncture of the Hungarian Soviet Republic.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the paradigm case for the "continuity-of-problem" position in the periodization debate: the pre-Marxist and Marxist Lukács are read as successive articulations of one underlying diagnosis (loss of totality/meaning under modernity), directly bearing on how the review should treat the relation between Theory of the Novel and the historical-materialist theory of history in HCC.

  POSITION: Continuity model ("romantic anti-capitalism" evolving into revolutionary Marxism); explicitly opposed to readings that treat 1918 as an epistemological break. First published 1979 (New Left Books); cited here in the 2023 Verso reissue, translated/edited by Patrick Camiller.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, romanticism, Bolshevism, continuity-thesis, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{aratobreines1979younglukacs,
  author = {Arato, Andrew and Breines, Paul},
  title = {The Young Lukács and the Origins of Western Marxism},
  year = {1979},
  publisher = {Pluto Press},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Arato and Breines argue that Western Marxism as a distinct tradition (as opposed to Second- and Third-International orthodoxy) originates in problems Lukács inherited from his pre-Marxist engagement with Simmel, Weber, and German Idealism -- above all the problem of "reification" understood as a successor category to the earlier aesthetic-existential problem of "soul and form." They present History and Class Consciousness as the point where these pre-Marxist categories are transposed onto the standpoint of the proletariat.

  RELEVANCE: Alongside Löwy, this is the founding statement of the continuity thesis and the source of the phrase "the young Lukács" as an analytic category; it directly shapes how commentators periodize the relationship between Theory of the Novel's philosophy of history and the mature theory of reification and totality.

  POSITION: Continuity model; credited (with Löwy) in later scholarship as having established "the Lukács question" as a genuine problem in the historiography of Western Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, Western-Marxism, continuity-thesis, reification, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{congdon1983younglukacs,
  author = {Congdon, Lee},
  title = {The Young Lukács},
  year = {1983},
  publisher = {University of North Carolina Press},
  address = {Chapel Hill},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on manuscripts and correspondence discovered in the Lukács Archive in 1972, Congdon presents the first archivally grounded intellectual biography of Lukács's pre-1918 years, arguing that his understanding of Simmel, Dostoevsky, and Hegel was shaped as much by personal crisis -- his relationships with Irma Seidler, Jelena Grabenko, and Gertrud Bortstieber -- as by abstract philosophical argument, and that the mature Lukács's trajectory "from unrestricted freedom to unrestricted despotism" reflects a personality that "hated injustice more than he loved human beings."

  RELEVANCE: Offers a biographically contingent counterweight to Löwy's and Arato/Breines's more philosophically deterministic continuity narratives; useful for testing whether the periodization of Lukács's thought should be explained by internal conceptual logic or external biographical circumstance.

  POSITION: Biographical/contingency model, skeptical of strong continuity or teleological readings of the pre-Marxist-to-Marxist transition.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, biography, archive, young-Lukacs, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{gluck1985lukacsgeneration,
  author = {Gluck, Mary},
  title = {Georg Lukács and His Generation, 1900-1918},
  year = {1985},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gluck situates the young Lukács within the "Sunday Circle" network of Budapest intellectuals (Karl Mannheim, Arnold Hauser, Béla Balázs, Anna Lesznai) and argues that revolutionary socialism was not the inevitable outcome of his early cultural radicalism but only one of several live options within the fragmented ideological climate of postwar Central Europe.

  RELEVANCE: Directly challenges teleological periodizations (continuity or rupture models alike) that read the 1918 conversion as philosophically predetermined by earlier commitments; reframes the "young Lukács" as embedded in a generational and institutional milieu rather than a self-contained philosophical trajectory.

  POSITION: Contingency/generational model; treats the conversion to Bolshevism as historically contingent rather than dialectically necessary.
  },
  keywords = {periodization, Sunday-Circle, biography, contingency, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{kadarkay1991lukacslife,
  author = {Kadarkay, Arpad},
